# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill it in.  `.env.local` is git-ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave DATABASE_URL unset and the app runs on an embedded PostgreSQL (PGlite)
# stored in `.data/pg`. That is the right choice for local development and the
# preview sandbox — no server to install, and accounts really persist.
#
# For production, point this at a hosted Postgres (Supabase, Neon, RDS, ...).
# Use the *pooled* connection string if your host offers one (Supabase: the
# "Transaction pooler" URI, port 6543) because serverless functions open a new
# connection per invocation. Paste `db/schema.sql` into the host's SQL editor
# first, or let the app create the tables on first request.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.PROJECT:PASSWORD@aws-0-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#
# Any of these may hold the connection string instead — the first one that is
# set wins, in this order:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  →  POSTGRES_URL  →  POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL  →  SUPABASE_DB_URL
#                 →  POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING
#
# The extra names matter on hosts whose own database integrations inject them:
# connecting a Supabase or Neon project from the Vercel dashboard sets
# POSTGRES_URL (pooled) and POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING (direct), not
# DATABASE_URL — so the app connects with nothing to copy. The pooled string is
# tried before the direct one, which is last because Supabase's direct host is
# IPv6-only and therefore cannot be reached from a host with no IPv6 route.
#
# To see which one a deployment is actually using, open /api/health on it: it
# reports the driver, the variable name, the host and the server version — plus
# a plain-language hint naming the fix when the connection fails. It never
# reports a user, a password or a database name.
#
# Never use Supabase's "Direct connection" string (db.PROJECT.supabase.co) on
# Vercel or any IPv4-only host: that hostname is IPv6-only, unreachable from
# there, and every sign-in fails with "check the database connection".

# Required only if you must run the embedded database in a production build.
# ALLOW_EMBEDDED_DB=1

# Overrides the embedded data directory (default `.data/pg`).
# PGLITE_DIR=.data/pg

# --- Supabase -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. The app talks to PostgreSQL directly (DATABASE_URL above), so these
# are only needed for Supabase features beyond the database - file storage for
# lesson uploads, for example. Find them in the Supabase dashboard under
# Project settings → API keys, and the URL under Project settings → Data API.
#
# The publishable key is meant to be public: it ships to browsers and row level
# security is what protects the data.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://abcdefghijklmnop.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxx
#
# The secret key bypasses row level security. Server-side only: never prefix it
# with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and never commit it.
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_xxx
#
# The Storage bucket that holds lesson videos, slide decks, posters and the
# owner's branding images. Create it once (Storage → New bucket, `lesson-files`
# is a good name), then put its name here. All three values must be set for
# object storage to be used; with any of them missing, files go to the local
# disk under .data/uploads instead.
# SUPABASE_BUCKET=lesson-files
#
# Is that bucket public? Click Storage → <bucket> → Make public in the dashboard,
# then set this. The app then hands the browser the bucket's permanent CDN URLs
# instead of creating a one-hour signed URL per view, so repeated views of a
# video are served from the edge rather than from Supabase's origin.
#
# Off (or unset) the app signs a URL for each view: slower for popular files, but
# the link expires and it works with a private bucket. Either way the routes in
# `/api/lesson-files/...` check the viewer's account, pass, purchase and lesson
# access before handing out any URL, so a locked video stays locked — but a public
# URL, unlike a signed one, keeps working for anyone it is forwarded to.
#
# SUPABASE_BUCKET_PUBLIC=1
#
# Check the storage round trip with `npm run storage:check` (it also reports
# whether this flag matches the bucket), and the key pairing with
# `npm run supabase:check`.

# --- Sessions --------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. Must be at least 32 characters. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# Unset in development, the app generates `.data/session-secret` once — set
# this in production, otherwise restarting with a fresh filesystem would sign
# everyone out.
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Owner account (the teacher) -------------------------------------------
# There are two roles: students and the owner. The owner is the teacher — the
# only account that can publish lessons, set prices and manage students. On the
# first request against an empty database these are used to create that one
# account; afterwards the password in the database wins, so you can change it
# from the account page and even delete these two variables.
#
# Leave both unset and /owner-sign-in offers a one-time "Set up the owner
# account" form on an empty database instead (useful locally). Set them for a
# deployment: the setup route refuses to run while they are configured.
OWNER_EMAIL=teacher@example.com
OWNER_PASSWORD=

# Display name for the owner, used in the console and the lesson byline.
# Defaults to the part of OWNER_EMAIL before the @ (so "teacher@..." becomes
# "Teacher"), which is rarely the name you want learners to see. Set it and it
# is kept in step with the account; leave it unset to manage the name from the
# account page instead.
OWNER_NAME=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — Mobile Money, cards and bank transfers via Paystack
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Students pay in Ghana cedis: MTN MoMo, Telecel Cash and AT Money (approved
# with the MoMo PIN on their own phone), plus cards and bank transfers on the
# same secure page. The PIN and card numbers never touch this app.
#
# Without a secret key the checkout runs in DEMO mode: the MoMo approval is
# simulated and no real money moves — the whole flow can still be tried end
# to end. Set the key (test first, live when ready) and the same checkout
# takes real payments.
#
# 1. Create a free account at https://paystack.com and activate GHS.
# 2. Copy the SECRET key from Settings → API keys (sk_test_… first).
# 3. In Paystack → Settings → Webhooks add:
#      https://your-domain.com/api/webhooks/paystack
#    so approvals confirm even if the student closes their browser.
# 4. Run `npm run payments:check` to confirm the key works.
#
PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=
#
# The public URL of this deployment, used as the base of the page Paystack
# returns the student to after payment. Leave unset locally (the request's
# own origin is used instead).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://codemasterghana.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Student email (receipts, course completion, certificates)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Students get an email when they buy something, when they finish a course, and
# when their certificate is issued (src/lib/email.ts). Sending is best-effort:
# if no provider is configured the email is logged to the server console and
# nothing breaks.
#
# Option 1 — Resend (recommended, no SMTP server needed):
#   RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxx
#   EMAIL_FROM="Codemaster Ghana <hello@yourdomain.com>"
#
# Option 2 — any SMTP server (Gmail app password, Mailgun, a VPS, ...):
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#   SMTP_PORT=587
#   SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
#   SMTP_PASSWORD=your-app-password
#   SMTP_SECURE=0            # 1 for port 465 / implicit TLS
#   EMAIL_FROM="Codemaster Ghana <you@gmail.com>"
#
# RESEND_API_KEY wins if both are set. EMAIL_FROM must use a domain the
# provider lets you send from.
RESEND_API_KEY=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_SECURE=0
EMAIL_FROM=
